INT. NIGHT FERRY - CAR DECK - NIGHT

Rows of parked cars lashed down with chains. Engine hum through the steel floor. Fog presses against the open bow, and somewhere out in the dark a horn answers the ship's horn.

HOLLIS BRANDVOLD, 40s, rumpled raincoat, a badge on his belt, walks the aisle. He stops beside a white sedan with its windows fogged from inside. He taps the glass.

The window lowers an inch. ZORAIDA KEMP, 30s, a scarf over her mouth, looks out with wide dark eyes.

HOLLIS
Ma'am. This deck should be empty.

ZORAIDA
I'm waiting for my husband.

HOLLIS
He's in the car?

ZORAIDA
He's on the upper deck.

HOLLIS
Then why are you down here?

She doesn't answer. Hollis steps back, hand drifting toward his coat. She opens the door and slides out, slowly, palms where he can see them. The scarf drops from her face.

HOLLIS (CONT'D)
(realizing)
You're the Kemp woman. From the bank.

ZORAIDA
Zoraida.

HOLLIS
You were supposed to be in Rotterdam.

ZORAIDA
I changed my mind.

HOLLIS
That's not how it works.

ZORAIDA
It's exactly how it works. That's why you're standing here.

She nods at the trunk of the sedan. He follows her glance, then fixes on her again.

HOLLIS
What's in the trunk?

ZORAIDA
The reason you were sent.

HOLLIS
Open it.

ZORAIDA
Detective, with respect, it's not that kind of trunk.

He draws the pistol from his coat and aims it at her chest. She doesn't flinch. Fog drifts between them. Behind him, a chain rattles in a swell, and he doesn't look.

HOLLIS
Open it.

ZORAIDA
You first.

HOLLIS
Excuse me?

ZORAIDA
You've been following me since Copenhagen. You've had a dozen chances. You're not here to arrest me.

HOLLIS
I'm here because I was told to be.

ZORAIDA
By whom?

HOLLIS
That's not your business.

ZORAIDA
It's exactly my business. It's the only business.

HOLLIS
Zoraida.

ZORAIDA
Say it, Hollis. Who sent you?

His jaw works. The gun wavers a fraction.

HOLLIS
Your husband.

ZORAIDA
(softly)
There it is.

HOLLIS
He said you'd run.

ZORAIDA
He said wrong.

She reaches into the sedan, pulls out a manila envelope, and tosses it at his feet. It lands with a slap. He stares at it.

ZORAIDA (CONT'D)
That's every account he's moved in six years. Every name. Every number.

HOLLIS
Why give it to me?

ZORAIDA
Because you're the first one who didn't kill me when he had the chance.

Hollis bends, slowly, without taking the gun off her. Picks up the envelope. Weighs it.

HOLLIS
If I take this, I'm a dead man.

ZORAIDA
If you don't, we both are.

A long moment. Then, overhead, a steward's footsteps on the grating. A cart rolls. A voice hums.

Hollis lowers the pistol. Tucks the envelope in his coat. Zoraida lets her breath out.

HOLLIS
Get in the car.

ZORAIDA
It's my car.

HOLLIS
I'm driving.

The ship's horn blasts. The deck shivers. Hollis opens the driver's door and climbs in.
